---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import MapLinks from '../components/MapLinks.astro';
import ArticleCard from '../components/ArticleCard.astro';
import PhotoGrid from '../components/PhotoGrid.astro';
import ChannelCards from '../components/ChannelCards.astro';
import StartHere from '../components/StartHere.astro';
import DiscordCTA from '../components/DiscordCTA.astro';
import Button from '../components/Button.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
import { publishedArticles } from '../lib/help';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
// The SDR waterfall lives outside src/assets/photos/ so the grid does not pick it up.
import sdrWaterfall from '../assets/sdr-waterfall.jpg';

const latest = (await publishedArticles()).slice(0, 3);

---

<BaseLayout title={site.name} description={site.tagline}>
  <Hero />

  <Section
    id="about"
    eyebrow="About"
    title="About SoCal Mesh"
    lede="No cell towers. No internet. Just radios, and the people who run them."
  >
    <div class="blocks">
      <article>
        <h3>What is SoCal Mesh?</h3>
        <p>
          SoCal Mesh is a community of individuals working to build a network of Meshtastic
          radios in Southern California. This network acts like an area-wide text messaging
          system, allowing people to communicate publicly or privately with anyone on the
          network. The mesh network can also be used to transmit GPS locations or read weather
          and other telemetry. All of this happens without any external infrastructure. No
          cell phone towers, no internet. We have hundreds of nodes and are growing every day.
          Please join us and feel free to ask questions on our{' '}
          <a href={site.discord.invite} rel="noopener">Discord</a>.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h3>What is Meshtastic?</h3>
        <p>
          Meshtastic is an open source, decentralized, off-grid mesh network. It is made up of
          small communicators that can form a mesh network. These communicators can be linked
          to a phone via Bluetooth or Wi-Fi. Meshtastic uses inexpensive LoRa radios as a
          long-range communication platform. Learn more at the{' '}
          <a href={site.meshtastic.site} rel="noopener">official Meshtastic website</a>.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article>
        <h3>How it works</h3>
        <p>
          People across Southern California run nodes. Some are at home, some sit on hilltops, some
          live in a backpack. The radios talk to each other over LoRa in the 915 MHz band used in
          the US. The exact frequency comes from the preset and frequency slot you set, which is why
          it matters that we all use the same ones. They mesh using the open source Meshtastic
          firmware. You pair your own node to your phone over Bluetooth or Wi-Fi, and the node
          handles the radio side. Messages hop from node to node, so the network reaches further
          than any single radio can, and it keeps working when one node drops off.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="channels"
    tone="raised"
    eyebrow="Channels"
    title="The two channels we run"
    lede="Start on LongFast, frequency slot 20. That is where many of us are. MediumFast slot 45 is an alternate worth trying once you are on the air."
  >
    <ChannelCards />

    <div class="why">
      <div>
        <h3>Start on LongFast, slot 20</h3>
        <p>
          It is where many of us are, and it is closest to what your radio already ships with, so it
          is the setting most likely to find you somebody on your first try. If you only ever change
          two things, make them the preset and the slot.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>MediumFast slot 45 is the alternate</h3>
        <p>
          Plenty of us in SoCal use it, and it is faster on the air. The same message takes less
          airtime, so a busy channel fills up more slowly. It is not always strong though, and
          whether it works for you depends on your area. Try it if you can connect on it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>How to tell what is near you</h3>
        <p>
          Check <a href={site.maps[0].url} rel="noopener">our live map</a> for MediumFast slot 45
          nodes around you. Remember the maps only show nodes that uplink to MQTT, so there are
          always more nodes on the air than any map displays. An empty patch is not proof nobody is
          there. Some of why MF45 can be weaker is physics: it has about 5 dB less link budget than
          LongFast, so each hop reaches less far and the channel leans on having enough nodes nearby.
          Asking on Discord is the surer answer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="sources">
      Where these numbers come from: Meshtastic's own{' '}
      <a href="https://meshtastic.org/docs/overview/radio-settings/" rel="noopener">radio settings
      reference</a>{' '}(the presets, their speeds and their link budgets). The case for MediumFast
      is argued by Meshtastic itself in{' '}
      <a href="https://meshtastic.org/blog/why-your-mesh-should-switch-from-longfast/" rel="noopener"
        >Why your mesh should switch from LongFast</a>, and by the communities that have moved.{' '}
      <a href="https://bayme.sh/docs/getting-started/recommended-settings/" rel="noopener">Bay Area
      Mesh</a> and <a href="https://mtnme.sh/mediumfast/" rel="noopener">MtnMe.sh</a> are both on
      MediumFast slot 45. It is a genuine argument. It just leans on having enough nodes near you,
      and that is the part that varies across Southern California.
    </p>

    <p class="warn">
      Set <strong>both</strong> the preset and the frequency slot. Type the slot number in rather
      than leaving it at 0. On 0 the radio works its frequency out from the primary channel's name,
      which is fine on a factory-fresh radio and stops being fine the moment anything has renamed
      that channel. A LongFast radio also cannot hear a MediumFast one at all, whatever the slot
      says, because the preset has to match too.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="start"
    eyebrow="New here?"
    title="Start here"
    lede="From an unopened radio to your first message on the mesh."
  >
    <StartHere />
    <p class="more">
      <Button href={site.discord.invite} variant="accent" external>Ask us on Discord</Button>
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="maps"
    tone="raised"
    eyebrow="Maps"
    title="See the network"
    lede="Start with ours. Read the caveat below before you judge coverage from any of them."
  >
    <MapLinks />

    <p class="warn">
      <strong>The maps show less than the network.</strong> They only show nodes that uplink to
      MQTT, which is the <strong>OK to MQTT</strong> setting, and plenty of people never turn it on.
      There are many more nodes on the air than any of these maps display. An empty area means
      nobody is uplinking there, not that nobody is there.
    </p>

    <p class="sources">
      They are also not one data source. Ours is our own{' '}
      <a href={site.github.meshview} rel="noopener">meshview</a> instance. Uplink to our MQTT
      server and you show up on <a href="https://meshmap.net" rel="noopener">meshmap.net</a> and{' '}
      <a href="https://meshtastic.liamcottle.net/" rel="noopener">Liam's map</a> as well, because
      we share to both of them. Each applies its own filters, so the odd node can still be missing.
      Of the two, meshmap is the one we have found most dependable, and it covers other regions as
      well.{' '}
      <a href="https://meshview.world/" rel="noopener">meshview.world</a> works differently again.
      Rather than taking our traffic, it gathers up the meshview instances it knows about, ours
      included, and shows them together. Getting onto the maps at all is covered in{' '}
      <a href={url('/help/how-do-i-uplink-to-mqtt/')}>How do I uplink to MQTT</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="help"
    eyebrow="Help"
    title="Getting started"
    lede="Short guides written by people in the mesh."
  >
    {
      latest.length > 0 ? (
        <>
          <div class="cards">
            {latest.map((article) => (
              <ArticleCard
                slug={article.id}
                title={article.data.title}
                description={article.data.description}
                author={article.data.author}
                date={article.data.date}
                updated={article.data.updated}
              />
            ))}
          </div>
          <p class="more">
            <a href={url('/help/')}>All help articles →</a>
          </p>
        </>
      ) : (
        <p class="lede">
          No articles yet. Ask on <a href={site.discord.invite} rel="noopener">Discord</a> and we
          will write one.
        </p>
      )
    }
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="photos"
    tone="raised"
    eyebrow="Photos"
    title="Out in the field"
    lede="Nodes, antennas, meetups, and a waterfall full of LoRa."
  >
    <PhotoGrid />
    <div class="sdr">
      <Image
        src={sdrWaterfall}
        alt="An SDR waterfall display showing bursts of LoRa traffic near 906.9 MHz"
        widths={[480, 800, 1200, 1600]}
        width={1600}
        height={900}
        sizes="(min-width: 64rem) 60rem, 92vw"
        format="webp"
        quality={72}
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <p class="caption">LoRa traffic on the 33 cm band, seen from an SDR.</p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="discord">
    <DiscordCTA />
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="community"
    tone="raised"
    eyebrow="Community"
    title="Projects and links"
    lede="Some of this we run. Some of it we simply depend on."
  >
    <ul class="links">
      {
        site.communityLinks.map((link) => (
          <li>
            <a href={link.url} rel="noopener">
              {link.name}
            </a>
            <p>{link.blurb}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>

    <p class="thanks">
      Thank you to the people who build these. Our map is an instance of{' '}
      <a href={site.github.meshview} rel="noopener">meshview</a>; the parrot in our Discord comes
      from <a href={site.github.parrot} rel="noopener">Rage Against the Meshine</a>; and{' '}
      <a href={site.github.meshSense} rel="noopener">MeshSense</a> is how a lot of us make sense of
      what is connected to what. None of these are ours, and we build a better mesh with them than
      we could without them.
    </p>

    <p class="more">
      <Button href={site.discord.invite} variant="accent" external>Join us on Discord</Button>
    </p>
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .blocks {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-l);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 20rem), 1fr));
  }

  .blocks h3 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-xs);
  }

  .blocks p {
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .cards {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-m);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 18rem), 1fr));
  }

  .more {
    margin-top: var(--space-m);
  }

  .sdr {
    margin-top: var(--space-m);
  }

  .sdr img {
    width: 100%;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .caption {
    margin-top: var(--space-xs);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--text-faint);
  }

  .why {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 17rem), 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-m);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-m);
  }

  .why h3 {
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
    font-size: var(--step-0);
  }

  .why p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .sources {
    max-width: 70ch;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-m);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  /* The credit to the projects we depend on. Deliberately full-size body text
     rather than the muted .sources treatment, because it is a thank-you and not a footnote. */
  .thanks {
    max-width: 70ch;
    margin-top: var(--space-l);
    padding-top: var(--space-m);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .warn {
    max-width: 70ch;
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--space-s) var(--space-m);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
    background: var(--bg-raised);
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .warn strong {
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .links {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-m);
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 18rem), 1fr));
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .links li {
    background: var(--bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--space-m);
  }

  .links a {
    font-weight: 650;
  }

  .links p {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xs);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }
</style>
